     kknnaappssaacckk is not itself an invokable command; it is the
     public-key knapsack encryption scheme, implemented by the
     routines in kknnaappssaacckk.cc, ppuubblliicc.cc, ppkkiioo.cc, and ggpppphh.cc, that
     underlies the eennrroollll, xxeennccooddee, and xxddeeccooddee commands.

     A user's public key is derived from a secret passphrase by
     the function kknnaappssaacckk(), which seeds a multiprecision
     linear-congruential random-number generator from the
     passphrase text and uses it to build a superincreasing knap-
     sack vector _k._d[_0.._6_3] together with a pair of modulus/mul-
     tiplier values (_k._m_1/_k._w_1 and _k._m_2/_k._w_2) and a random shuf-
     fle order _k._s_h_u_f_l[].  ppuubblliicc() then walks the knapsack
     through the two modulus/multiplier transformations, shuf-
     fling the terms into _k_e_y[_0.._6_3] by _k._s_h_u_f_l[], to produce the
     public key.

     The public key is stored, one 64-bit component per line in a
     16-byte-per-element binary format, in a file named for the
     user under /uussrr/ssppooooll/ppuubbkkeeyy, where anyone may read it to
     encrypt a message to that user; only the passphrase holder
     can regenerate the matching private knapsack, so only that
     user can decrypt it.

     eennrroollll
          Enroll or re-key a user.  Called with no argument, it
          enrolls (or re-keys) the caller, as found by ggeett-
          llooggiinn(); called as eennrroollll _u_s_e_r (normally only useful
          for the superuser), it enrolls the named _u_s_e_r. If the
          user is already enrolled, eennrroollll first asks for the
          current passphrase and verifies it by recomputing the
          public key and comparing every component against the
          stored file before asking for a new passphrase (twice,
          to catch typing mistakes).  Entering an empty new
          passphrase removes the user's public-key file,
          withdrawing the user from the scheme.

     xxeennccooddee
          Read standard input and write it, encrypted, to stan-
          dard output, in a mail-compatible text format (base-95
          digits, one block per line).  Called with no argument,
          it prompts for a passphrase and builds a public key
          from it on the fly; called as xxeennccooddee _u_s_e_r it reads
          _u_s_e_r's stored public key instead and does not prompt.

